==English==

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|dilatorie}}, from {{der|en|fro|dilatoire}},<ref>{{R:Etymonline}}</ref> from {{der|en|la|dīlātōrius||extending or putting off (time)}}, from {{m|la|dīlātor}}, from {{m|la|differō}}. Not etymologically related to {{m|en|delay}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|en|/ˈdɪlət(ə)ɹi/|a=RP}}
** {{qualifier|rarely}} {{IPA|en|/daɪˈleɪt(ə)ɹi/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-dilatory.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈdɪləˌtɔɹi/|a=GA}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Naomi Persephone Amethyst (NaomiAmethyst)-dilatory.wav|a=US}}

====Adjective====
{{en-adj|-}}

# Intentionally [[delay]]ing (someone or something), intended to cause delay, [[gain]] time, or [[adjourn]] decision.
#: ''a '''dilatory''' [[strategy]]''
#* {{RQ:Motley Dutch Republic|passage=Alva, as usual, brought his '''dilatory''' policy to bear upon his adversary with great effect.}}
# [[slow|Slow]] or [[tardy]].

